      "hints": [
        "Express $u_3 = ar^2$ and $u_6 = ar^5$, then solve $ar^2 = 8ar^5$ for $r$.",
        "Use $S_6 = \\frac{a(1-r^6)}{1-r} = 31.5$ with $r = \\frac{1}{2}$.",
        "Solve for $a$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Use the relationship between the 3rd and 6th terms of the GP to solve for the common ratio $r$:\n$$u_3 = ar^2, \\quad u_6 = ar^5$$\n$$ar^2 = 8ar^5 \\implies 1 = 8r^3 \\implies r^3 = \\frac{1}{8} \\implies r = \\frac{1}{2}$$",
        "Set up the sum formula $S_6$ for the first six terms with $r = \\frac{1}{2}$:\n$$S_6 = \\frac{a\\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^6\\right)}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{a\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{64}\\right)}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 2a\\left(\\frac{63}{64}\\right) = \\frac{63a}{32}$$",
        "Equate $S_6$ to $31\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{63}{2}$ and solve for the first term $a$:\n$$\\frac{63a}{32} = \\frac{63}{2} \\implies \\frac{a}{32} = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies a = 16$$",
        "State the first term of the progression:\n$$a = 16$$"
      ],
